@attribute [Route($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_Components}/{GlobalValues.Component_Path_Part_Tabs}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Accessibility}")]

<PageTitle>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_Tabs @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Accessibility</PageTitle>

<h1>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_Tabs @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Accessibility</h1>
<p>
    This Tabs component was built following the W3C ARIA Authoring Practices Guide (APG). By adhering to standardised patterns, we ensure that the component
    behaves predictably across different browsers and assistive technologies.
</p>
<p>
    Each Tab has a <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">HasPanelTabIndex</span> parameter, which by default is true. When true a <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">tabindex="0"</span> is added to the associated Tab Panel.
    If you know there will be no focusable elements in the panel or the panel is scrollable then you should leave this set to true. 
    Without this, when the user is on a Tab and presses the Tab key they would go past the panel and onto the next focusable element on the page and/or may not be able to scroll the panel content.
</p>
<p>
    If you know there will be a focusable element at the start of the Tab Panel content or there is a heading at the start of the panel and it's not scrollable, 
    then you can set the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">HasPanelTabIndex</span> parameter to false to omit the tabindex on the Tab Panel.
</p>
<p>
    If the first focusable element is in the middle of the panel, past important content, then I would retain the tabindex on the Tab Panel, so a screen reader user can read from the start of the panel content.
</p>
<h2 id="tabs-keyboard">Keyboard interaction</h2>
<ul aria-labelledby="tabs-keyboard" class="keyboard-list">
    <li>
        <kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Tab</kbd> - when focus moves into the tab list, places focus on the active tab element
        <br />When the tab list contains the focus, moves focus to the next element in the page tab sequence outside the tablist, which is the
        tabpanel unless the first element containing meaningful content inside the tabpanel is focusable.
    </li>
    <li><kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Left Arrow</kbd> - moves focus to the previous tab. If the focus is on the first tab, moves focus to the last tab.</li>
    <li><kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Right Arrow</kbd> - moves focus to the next tab. If the focus is on the last tab, moves focus to the first tab.</li>
    <li><kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Enter or Space</kbd> - activates the tab if it was not activated automatically on focus..</li>
    <li><kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Home</kbd> - moves focus to the first tab.</li>
    <li><kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">End</kbd> - moves focus to the last tab..</li>
</ul>
<p>
    <strong>Note: </strong>These keyboard interactions are primarily aimed at keyboard-only users. Screen reader users will often navigate differently using their assistive technology's
    built-in commands, though the patterns also apply when a screen reader is operating in interaction mode.
</p>

<h2 id="device-testing">Accessibility device testing</h2>

<ul aria-labelledby="device-testing">
    <li>JAWS, Narrator and NVDA on Windows were each paired with every one of the following browsers: Google Chrome, Microsoft Edge and Mozilla Firefox.</li>
    <li>VoiceOver on macOS was paired with Apple's Safari.</li>
    <li>VoiceOver on iPhone was paired with mobile Safari.</li>
    <li>Talkback on Android was paired with mobile Google Chrome.</li>
    <li>Voice access on Windows 11</li>
</ul>

<p>
    <strong>Note: </strong> Testing was performed with the latest browser and assistive technologies versions that were available at the time of testing (see release history on the installation page for approximate testing dates)
</p>

<h2 id="screen-reader-quirks">Screen reader quirks or known issues</h2>
<ul aria-labelledby="screen-reader-quirks">
    <li>
        <b>Narrator</b> generally will not turn off scan mode when landing on the tab list required for the supported keys.
        Note, using the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">Up Arrow </span>and <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">Down Arrow</span> keys will navigate the tabs but this requires the user to select the item with the
        <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">Enter or Space</span> key like manual activation.
    </li>
    <li><b>Narrator</b> on all browsers. When the tabs component uses automatic activation and you turn scan mode off enabling Narrator to use the supported keys, it announces the selected tab title twice.</li>
    <li><b>NVDA</b> on all browsers. When the tabs component uses automatic activation, although NVDA switches automatically to focus mode and appears to only announce the select tab title once, in the speech viewer its listed twice.</li>
</ul>

<p>
    <strong>Note: </strong> The same quirks were also noted on the APG example.
</p>

<div class="flex-content justify-content-between">
    <a class="@GlobalValues.Link_Button_Class @GlobalValues.Link_Button_Left_Arrow_Modifier" href="@($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_Components}/{GlobalValues.Component_Path_Part_Tabs}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Installation}")">
        <span aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_Tabs - @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Installation</span>
        <span aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </a>
    <a class="@GlobalValues.Link_Button_Class @GlobalValues.Link_Button_Right_Arrow_Modifier" href="@($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_Components}/{GlobalValues.Component_Path_Part_Tabs}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Api}")">
        <span aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_Tabs - @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Api</span>
        <span aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </a>
</div>
